{% comment %} Get cart size first - assign takes filters, if takes operators {% endcomment %}
{% assign cart_count = {{custom_attribute.${cart_items}}} | size %}

{% if cart_count > 3 %}
  <div style="background: #f5f5f5; padding: 20px; margin: 20px 0;">
    <h3>Your cart is filling up!</h3>

    {% for item in {{custom_attribute.${cart_items}}} limit: 3 %}
      <div style="margin: 10px 0;">
        {{item.name | default: 'Item'}} — {{item.price | money}}
      </div>
    {% endfor %}

    {% comment %} Two custom attributes can't be referenced in one expression {% endcomment %}
    {% assign rewards = {{custom_attribute.${rewards}}} | default: 0 %}
    {% assign total_balance = {{custom_attribute.${giftcard}}} | default: 0 | plus: rewards %}

    <p style="margin-top: 15px; font-weight: bold;">
      Your total balance: {{total_balance | money}}
    </p>
  </div>
{% endif %}
```

This assumes `cart_items`, `rewards`, and `giftcard` are all custom attributes. If any are standard attributes, remove the `custom_attribute.` prefix.

**On the money amounts:** Braze's `money` filter does NOT divide by 100 — so if your prices are stored in cents (e.g. `14500` for $145.00), add `| divided_by: 100.00` before `| money`.

